description.narrative:
Basic sciences and engineering constitute the core of modern science, and they are, and will still be, at the root of any innovative science answer to meet basic human needs and solve pressing challenges of sustainable development. From quality science education at all academic levels to building institutional research capacity, to promoting and catalysing international scientific collaboration and networks, to facilitating access to scientific information in particular through open science and open access to scientific information, UNESCO’s work in capacity development is at the heart of its mandate in science. Under the umbrella of the International Decade of Sciences for Sustainable Development (IDSSD), UNESCO will therefore continue to implement its capacity-building mandate in the basic sciences and engineering.

Objectives:
•	Establish North-South (N-S) or North-South-South (N-S-S) networks or collaborations through specialised centres and research networks in science and engineering under the International Decade framework. This will be achieved through a close collaboration between member countries, Centers of Excellence, UNESCO Category 1 institutes and Category 2 centres, UNESCO Chairs, the UNITWIN networks, the International Science Council, national academies, and the private sector. Moreover, the network of expertise will be enriched through the International Basic Sciences Programme’s experts who represent all electoral groups and a diverse range of scientific fields.

Key Activities:
•	The International Decade of Sciences for Sustainable Development (2024-2033) has been proclaimed by the UNGA in August 2023. As the lead agency of the Decade, UNESCO will work closely with Member States, UN agencies, international scientific unions, science academies, private sectors and the general public to develop the implementation plan and strategies, organise the opening, as well as resource mobilisation and initiation of projects/programmes under the umbrella of the decade in 2024-2025. Events and activities for International Days will also be celebrated under the Decade;
•	Raise awareness of the important role of basic sciences for SDGs and promote international dialogues and cooperations under the umbrella of IDSSD;
•	Provide institutional and human capacity-building opportunities for Member States to enhance their capacities in addressing global challenges such as Climate Change, lack of scientific infrastructure, etc., as well as for young and women scientists and engineers, with a focus on African countries;
•	Continue to develop partnerships and networks, engage more partners and stakeholders and conduct resource mobilisation to contribute to UNESCO's basic sciences and engineering programme;
•	Supporting, strengthening and extending its network of research institutes and affiliated Centres, including Category 2 Centres, Chairs, and intersectoral collaboration. Engage the network to contribute to both basic sciences and engineering programmes.
•	Through the International Center for Theoretical Physics (ICTP) and TWAS, foster research and capacity-building to advance scientific expertise in developing countries including through short and long-term education and training programmes; promote scientific capacity and excellence in the South through research grants, fellowships, exchange/mobility programmes, awards, TWAS Young Affiliates Network, and with OWSD investing specifically in supporting women scientists in the Global South.
•	Underscore the crucial role of basic sciences in providing science and technology-based solutions to address climate change issues and health, and food security.
